1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,530
The International Space Station weighs about four

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:05,907
hundred fifty thousand kilograms,

3
00:00:05,907 --> 00:00:09,005
and it floats about four hundred kilometers

4
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:10,158
above your head.

5
00:00:10,338 --> 00:00:13,100
It is bigger than a football field,

6
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:14,599
and it never lands.

7
00:00:14,779 --> 00:00:16,880
So why doesn't it fall down?

8
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:18,227
It does fall,

9
00:00:18,227 --> 00:00:19,394
all the time.

10
00:00:19,574 --> 00:00:21,408
It just keeps missing the ground.

11
00:00:21,588 --> 00:00:24,658
Today, ten parts of the space station,

12
00:00:24,658 --> 00:00:27,324
and the reason each one is there.

13
00:00:27,674 --> 00:00:29,920
There is no magic up there,

14
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:32,167
and no escape from gravity.

15
00:00:32,347 --> 00:00:34,345
At the height of the station,

16
00:00:34,345 --> 00:00:36,827
gravity is still almost as strong as

17
00:00:36,827 --> 00:00:37,792
on the ground.

18
00:00:37,972 --> 00:00:40,561
What the station has is speed,

19
00:00:40,561 --> 00:00:43,322
and a long list of machines that

20
00:00:43,322 --> 00:00:45,825
keep it alive in a place with

21
00:00:45,825 --> 00:00:48,328
no air, no shade and no help.

22
00:00:48,678 --> 00:00:51,702
As always, every fact here comes from

23
00:00:51,702 --> 00:00:52,928
a named source,

24
00:00:52,928 --> 00:00:56,361
NASA documents and spaceflight references,

25
00:00:56,361 --> 00:00:58,813
all listed in the description.

26
00:00:58,993 --> 00:01:01,568
Let's start with the biggest question of

27
00:01:01,568 --> 00:01:01,826
all.

28
00:01:02,176 --> 00:01:04,915
Why doesn't the space station fall down?

29
00:01:05,095 --> 00:01:08,235
Because it is moving sideways at about

30
00:01:08,235 --> 00:01:12,036
seven point six seven kilometers every second,

31
00:01:12,036 --> 00:01:16,415
twenty-seven thousand six hundred kilometers an hour.

32
00:01:16,595 --> 00:01:18,705
It does fall toward Earth,

33
00:01:18,705 --> 00:01:21,788
but the Earth's surface curves away as

34
00:01:21,788 --> 00:01:23,898
fast as the station drops.

35
00:01:24,078 --> 00:01:26,585
So it falls around the planet,

36
00:01:26,585 --> 00:01:27,754
over and over,

37
00:01:27,754 --> 00:01:31,681
completing a lap in about ninety-three minutes,

38
00:01:31,681 --> 00:01:34,689
fifteen and a half orbits every day.

39
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:36,967
Think of throwing a ball.

40
00:01:37,147 --> 00:01:38,888
The harder you throw,

41
00:01:38,888 --> 00:01:40,630
the farther it lands.

42
00:01:40,810 --> 00:01:42,312
Throw it hard enough,

43
00:01:42,312 --> 00:01:44,959
and the ground curves away beneath it

44
00:01:44,959 --> 00:01:46,104
before it lands.

45
00:01:46,284 --> 00:01:47,468
That is an orbit.

46
00:01:47,648 --> 00:01:50,970
The astronauts feel weightless not because gravity

47
00:01:50,970 --> 00:01:53,163
is gone, but because they and the

48
00:01:53,163 --> 00:01:55,090
station are falling together.

49
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:58,394
And this was done with an enormous

50
00:01:58,394 --> 00:02:00,566
machine, built in pieces,

51
00:02:00,566 --> 00:02:02,999
in orbit, by many countries.

52
00:02:03,179 --> 00:02:06,950
Humans have lived aboard continuously since the

53
00:02:06,950 --> 00:02:08,475
second of November,

54
00:02:08,475 --> 00:02:09,518
two thousand.

55
00:02:09,698 --> 00:02:11,867
Everything we will look at today is

56
00:02:11,867 --> 00:02:13,541
part of keeping that going.

57
00:02:13,891 --> 00:02:16,648
Why does the station have such enormous

58
00:02:16,648 --> 00:02:17,072
wings?

59
00:02:17,252 --> 00:02:18,641
They are solar arrays,

60
00:02:18,641 --> 00:02:20,410
and there are eight of them.

61
00:02:20,590 --> 00:02:23,924
Each one is thirty-five meters long and

62
00:02:23,924 --> 00:02:25,548
twelve meters wide,

63
00:02:25,548 --> 00:02:29,310
and each makes close to thirty-one kilowatts

64
00:02:29,310 --> 00:02:30,934
in direct sunlight.

65
00:02:31,114 --> 00:02:34,124
All eight together make about two hundred

66
00:02:34,124 --> 00:02:36,106
forty kilowatts in the sun,

67
00:02:36,106 --> 00:02:37,207
and on average,

68
00:02:37,207 --> 00:02:39,336
a bit less than half of that,

69
00:02:39,336 --> 00:02:40,878
because of the shade.

70
00:02:41,228 --> 00:02:42,921
Every ninety minute lap,

71
00:02:42,921 --> 00:02:46,237
the station spends about thirty-five minutes in

72
00:02:46,237 --> 00:02:47,578
the Earth's shadow.

73
00:02:47,758 --> 00:02:49,225
During that time,

74
00:02:49,225 --> 00:02:50,951
batteries take over.

75
00:02:51,131 --> 00:02:54,385
The battery system goes through sixteen charge

76
00:02:54,385 --> 00:02:56,578
and discharge cycles every day,

77
00:02:56,578 --> 00:02:58,983
which is why the batteries are one

78
00:02:58,983 --> 00:03:00,963
of the parts the crew has to

79
00:03:00,963 --> 00:03:02,590
replace over the years.

80
00:03:02,940 --> 00:03:04,431
Folded for launch,

81
00:03:04,431 --> 00:03:06,751
each wing fits in a box just

82
00:03:06,751 --> 00:03:08,988
fifty-one centimeters high.

83
00:03:09,168 --> 00:03:11,938
In space, it unfolds into a field

84
00:03:11,938 --> 00:03:14,876
of solar cells bigger than a tennis

85
00:03:14,876 --> 00:03:15,380
court.

86
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:17,317
It is a power plant,

87
00:03:17,317 --> 00:03:19,338
delivered in a shoebox,

88
00:03:19,338 --> 00:03:21,974
that has to work in vacuum for

89
00:03:21,974 --> 00:03:22,677
decades.

90
00:03:23,027 --> 00:03:25,697
Why do the solar wings keep turning?

91
00:03:25,877 --> 00:03:27,351
To follow the Sun.

92
00:03:27,531 --> 00:03:30,882
The station circles the Earth every ninety

93
00:03:30,882 --> 00:03:33,675
minutes, so the Sun appears to move

94
00:03:33,675 --> 00:03:35,670
across the sky very fast.

95
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:38,835
A rotating joint called the alpha gimbal

96
00:03:38,835 --> 00:03:40,924
turns the arrays to face it,

97
00:03:40,924 --> 00:03:42,342
and a second joint,

98
00:03:42,342 --> 00:03:43,536
the beta gimbal,

99
00:03:43,536 --> 00:03:46,148
adjusts for the angle of the orbit.

100
00:03:46,328 --> 00:03:48,707
A solar panel that does not face

101
00:03:48,707 --> 00:03:50,937
the Sun makes much less power.

102
00:03:51,287 --> 00:03:54,064
The station keeps its body pointed at

103
00:03:54,064 --> 00:03:56,616
the Earth, while its wings turn to

104
00:03:56,616 --> 00:03:57,742
follow the Sun.

105
00:03:57,922 --> 00:04:01,163
Those two jobs need different directions,

106
00:04:01,163 --> 00:04:03,377
so the joints do the work of

107
00:04:03,377 --> 00:04:05,037
a sunflower, all day,

108
00:04:05,037 --> 00:04:05,828
every day.

109
00:04:06,178 --> 00:04:08,521
Each of those joints is a moving

110
00:04:08,521 --> 00:04:10,864
part in a machine that is almost

111
00:04:10,864 --> 00:04:12,401
impossible to repair,

112
00:04:12,401 --> 00:04:14,671
which is why they are among the

113
00:04:14,671 --> 00:04:17,161
most watched parts of the station.

114
00:04:17,341 --> 00:04:19,728
A joint that sticks means a wing

115
00:04:19,728 --> 00:04:21,892
that stops making full power.

116
00:04:22,242 --> 00:04:25,091
Why does the station have giant white

117
00:04:25,091 --> 00:04:27,478
panels next to its solar wings?

118
00:04:27,658 --> 00:04:28,935
They are radiators.

119
00:04:29,115 --> 00:04:32,697
Every computer, light and astronaut makes heat,

120
00:04:32,697 --> 00:04:34,831
and in space there is no air

121
00:04:34,831 --> 00:04:36,127
to carry it away.

122
00:04:36,307 --> 00:04:38,228
The only way to get rid of

123
00:04:38,228 --> 00:04:40,445
heat in a vacuum is to radiate

124
00:04:40,445 --> 00:04:41,997
it as infrared light,

125
00:04:41,997 --> 00:04:45,395
so the station circulates liquid through pipes

126
00:04:45,395 --> 00:04:46,430
to the panels,

127
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:49,090
which shine the heat out into space.

128
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:52,369
The truss that carries the solar arrays

129
00:04:52,369 --> 00:04:54,397
also carries the radiators.

130
00:04:54,577 --> 00:04:57,430
Together, the two sets of panels are

131
00:04:57,430 --> 00:05:00,679
the station's lungs and its power supply,

132
00:05:00,679 --> 00:05:03,532
one bringing energy in and the other

133
00:05:03,532 --> 00:05:05,038
letting energy out.

134
00:05:05,388 --> 00:05:07,245
It is a strange problem.

135
00:05:07,425 --> 00:05:09,891
The danger in space is not only

136
00:05:09,891 --> 00:05:10,606
the cold.

137
00:05:10,786 --> 00:05:13,007
A station full of equipment,

138
00:05:13,007 --> 00:05:14,514
parked in sunlight,

139
00:05:14,514 --> 00:05:16,417
can get too hot to work.

140
00:05:16,597 --> 00:05:19,461
Keeping cool is as hard as keeping

141
00:05:19,461 --> 00:05:19,883
warm.

142
00:05:20,233 --> 00:05:22,650
Why does the station have a giant

143
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:23,530
metal spine?

144
00:05:23,710 --> 00:05:27,351
It is called the Integrated Truss Structure,

145
00:05:27,351 --> 00:05:29,171
built of ten segments,

146
00:05:29,171 --> 00:05:32,150
about one hundred eight meters long.

147
00:05:32,330 --> 00:05:34,747
It is the backbone that holds the

148
00:05:34,747 --> 00:05:36,724
solar arrays and radiators,

149
00:05:36,724 --> 00:05:39,434
far enough from the living modules so

150
00:05:39,434 --> 00:05:41,777
the wings do not shade the crew,

151
00:05:41,777 --> 00:05:42,802
or each other.

152
00:05:43,153 --> 00:05:45,325
A truss is a very light way

153
00:05:45,325 --> 00:05:46,612
to be very long.

154
00:05:46,792 --> 00:05:48,857
It is made of simple bars,

155
00:05:48,857 --> 00:05:51,796
with mostly empty space between them.

156
00:05:51,976 --> 00:05:54,775
That lets the station carry large wings

157
00:05:54,775 --> 00:05:57,215
and panels without the weight of a

158
00:05:57,215 --> 00:06:00,444
solid beam, which matters when every kilogram

159
00:06:00,444 --> 00:06:02,669
has to be launched on a rocket.

160
00:06:03,019 --> 00:06:06,179
The truss was assembled piece by piece,

161
00:06:06,179 --> 00:06:08,610
over years of shuttle flights,

162
00:06:08,610 --> 00:06:11,122
by astronauts doing spacewalks.

163
00:06:11,302 --> 00:06:13,682
It is one of the largest structures

164
00:06:13,682 --> 00:06:16,469
humans have ever built outside the Earth.

165
00:06:16,819 --> 00:06:18,924
Why does the station have a dome

166
00:06:18,924 --> 00:06:19,976
made of windows?

167
00:06:20,156 --> 00:06:21,881
It is called the Cupola,

168
00:06:21,881 --> 00:06:23,677
and it has seven windows,

169
00:06:23,677 --> 00:06:25,833
with a round one in the middle

170
00:06:25,833 --> 00:06:28,132
about eighty centimeters across.

171
00:06:28,312 --> 00:06:30,562
It is the control room for the

172
00:06:30,562 --> 00:06:33,711
robotic arm, a place from which astronauts

173
00:06:33,711 --> 00:06:36,260
can see the outside of the station

174
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:38,959
and the spacecraft arriving to dock.

175
00:06:39,309 --> 00:06:40,756
Moving a robot arm,

176
00:06:40,756 --> 00:06:43,269
or catching a cargo ship with it,

177
00:06:43,269 --> 00:06:45,706
is much easier when you can look

178
00:06:45,706 --> 00:06:47,610
at it with your own eyes.

179
00:06:47,790 --> 00:06:50,453
Cameras help, but a window gives the

180
00:06:50,453 --> 00:06:53,043
view and the depth that screens can

181
00:06:53,043 --> 00:06:53,339
not.

182
00:06:53,519 --> 00:06:55,893
It is also where astronauts go to

183
00:06:55,893 --> 00:06:57,188
look at the Earth.

184
00:06:57,538 --> 00:07:00,542
The windows are protected by shutters,

185
00:07:00,542 --> 00:07:03,072
closed when they are not in use,

186
00:07:03,072 --> 00:07:05,444
against dust and tiny impacts.

187
00:07:05,624 --> 00:07:08,554
A big window on a spacecraft is

188
00:07:08,554 --> 00:07:11,673
a risk, so it needs good reasons.

189
00:07:11,853 --> 00:07:13,793
The view of the arm and the

190
00:07:13,793 --> 00:07:14,871
planet was one.

191
00:07:15,221 --> 00:07:17,828
How does the station keep itself pointed

192
00:07:17,828 --> 00:07:20,109
the right way without burning fuel?

193
00:07:20,289 --> 00:07:23,037
With four giant spinning wheels,

194
00:07:23,037 --> 00:07:25,870
called control moment gyroscopes,

195
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:27,673
mounted on the truss.

196
00:07:27,853 --> 00:07:30,501
Tilt a spinning wheel and it pushes

197
00:07:30,501 --> 00:07:32,013
back on the station,

198
00:07:32,013 --> 00:07:32,846
turning it.

199
00:07:33,026 --> 00:07:35,428
They keep the station at a steady

200
00:07:35,428 --> 00:07:36,812
angle to the Earth,

201
00:07:36,812 --> 00:07:39,579
and they use only electricity from the

202
00:07:39,579 --> 00:07:40,526
solar arrays.

203
00:07:40,876 --> 00:07:43,569
Rocket thrusters can do the same job,

204
00:07:43,569 --> 00:07:46,190
but they burn propellant that has to

205
00:07:46,190 --> 00:07:47,865
be launched from Earth.

206
00:07:48,045 --> 00:07:49,375
Over the years,

207
00:07:49,375 --> 00:07:52,123
using the gyroscopes for turns,

208
00:07:52,123 --> 00:07:56,113
including turns before Soyuz capsules undock,

209
00:07:56,113 --> 00:07:58,772
has saved a lot of propellant.

210
00:07:59,122 --> 00:08:01,166
The gyroscopes have a limit.

211
00:08:01,346 --> 00:08:03,580
If they keep absorbing push,

212
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:05,575
they eventually saturate,

213
00:08:05,575 --> 00:08:08,048
and then the station has to use

214
00:08:08,048 --> 00:08:10,363
another way to get rid of the

215
00:08:10,363 --> 00:08:13,714
extra momentum, like thrusters or the pull

216
00:08:13,714 --> 00:08:15,150
of gravity itself.

217
00:08:15,330 --> 00:08:17,786
Even a machine without fuel has a

218
00:08:17,786 --> 00:08:18,233
limit.

219
00:08:18,582 --> 00:08:21,280
How does the station survive the pieces

220
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:23,424
of space junk flying around it?

221
00:08:23,604 --> 00:08:24,997
With shields.

222
00:08:25,177 --> 00:08:27,728
The walls of many modules have an

223
00:08:27,728 --> 00:08:29,428
aluminum outer bumper,

224
00:08:29,428 --> 00:08:31,979
and behind it a blanket of Nextel

225
00:08:31,979 --> 00:08:33,988
ceramic fabric and Kevlar,

226
00:08:33,988 --> 00:08:35,998
with the real wall behind.

227
00:08:36,178 --> 00:08:38,609
The design is meant to stop an

228
00:08:38,609 --> 00:08:41,852
aluminum ball about one centimeter wide,

229
00:08:41,852 --> 00:08:45,175
at speeds of several kilometers a second.

230
00:08:45,525 --> 00:08:48,521
The idea is called a Whipple shield.

231
00:08:48,701 --> 00:08:51,178
The outer bumper does not stop the

232
00:08:51,178 --> 00:08:51,615
piece.

233
00:08:51,795 --> 00:08:52,992
It breaks it up,

234
00:08:52,992 --> 00:08:55,013
melts it or turns it into a

235
00:08:55,013 --> 00:08:56,809
cloud of fine fragments,

236
00:08:56,809 --> 00:08:59,727
and the layers behind spread and absorb

237
00:08:59,727 --> 00:09:00,700
what is left.

238
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:02,965
A thin plate with a gap works

239
00:09:02,965 --> 00:09:04,978
better than one thick plate.

240
00:09:05,328 --> 00:09:07,019
The faster the object,

241
00:09:07,019 --> 00:09:08,556
the better it works.

242
00:09:08,736 --> 00:09:11,180
At several kilometers a second,

243
00:09:11,180 --> 00:09:14,254
a small fragment vaporizes when it hits

244
00:09:14,254 --> 00:09:15,122
the bumper.

245
00:09:15,302 --> 00:09:17,937
It is a strange rule of space.

246
00:09:18,117 --> 00:09:20,312
A fast thing can be easier to

247
00:09:20,312 --> 00:09:21,902
stop than a slow one.

248
00:09:22,251 --> 00:09:24,980
Why does a station need engines if

249
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:26,826
it is already in orbit?

250
00:09:27,006 --> 00:09:29,351
Because there is still a very thin

251
00:09:29,351 --> 00:09:32,110
trace of air at four hundred kilometers,

252
00:09:32,110 --> 00:09:34,041
and it drags on the station.

253
00:09:34,221 --> 00:09:37,748
Without help, the station loses about two

254
00:09:37,748 --> 00:09:40,758
kilometers of altitude every month.

255
00:09:40,938 --> 00:09:43,039
So, from time to time,

256
00:09:43,039 --> 00:09:45,140
a visiting spacecraft,

257
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:47,910
or the station's own engines,

258
00:09:47,910 --> 00:09:50,203
fire to lift it back up.

259
00:09:50,552 --> 00:09:52,190
Without those pushes,

260
00:09:52,190 --> 00:09:54,529
the orbit would slowly shrink,

261
00:09:54,529 --> 00:09:57,805
and eventually the station would fall into

262
00:09:57,805 --> 00:09:59,910
the atmosphere and burn up.

263
00:10:00,090 --> 00:10:02,419
The station is not simply parked up

264
00:10:02,419 --> 00:10:02,818
there.

265
00:10:02,998 --> 00:10:05,124
It is slowly being held up,

266
00:10:05,124 --> 00:10:06,542
month after month,

267
00:10:06,542 --> 00:10:09,455
by people on the ground planning each

268
00:10:09,455 --> 00:10:09,848
burn.

269
00:10:10,198 --> 00:10:12,500
So the answer to our title is

270
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:14,564
a little more complicated.

271
00:10:14,744 --> 00:10:17,697
The station does not fall down because

272
00:10:17,697 --> 00:10:19,951
it is always moving sideways,

273
00:10:19,951 --> 00:10:23,370
very fast, and because someone keeps topping

274
00:10:23,370 --> 00:10:24,380
up its speed.

275
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:26,685
Gravity never lets go.

276
00:10:26,865 --> 00:10:28,061
We just keep winning.

277
00:10:28,410 --> 00:10:30,861
Why does the station have a giant

278
00:10:30,861 --> 00:10:31,603
robot arm?

279
00:10:31,783 --> 00:10:33,300
It is Canadarm two,

280
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:35,696
and it is the station's crane.

281
00:10:35,876 --> 00:10:38,239
It moved the pieces of the station

282
00:10:38,239 --> 00:10:40,116
into place during assembly,

283
00:10:40,116 --> 00:10:42,687
and since then it has caught visiting

284
00:10:42,687 --> 00:10:45,537
cargo ships and moved astronauts to their

285
00:10:45,537 --> 00:10:46,302
work sites.

286
